  "notes": "Dual curve: EURIBOR projection discounted on â‚¬STR. ISDA standard: 1Y swap vs 3M Euribor, >1Y vs 6M. Fixed leg 30/360 bond basis. HICP breakevens as analytics ladder. TARGET calendar. FRA strip added 2026-07 (composite-only full securities) to shape the front end between swap pillars; FRAs whose maturity clashes with a quoted pillar are skipped by the bootstrapper. EUR FRA pillars REMOVED 2026-07: EUFR0I1C/011F prints sit ~10bp above the (far more liquid) EUSA-implied forwards and sawtooth the curve - 1y2y went +11bp vs FWCM. EUR front end stays swap-pillar based; do not re-add without checking the FRA source quality. S0201 FWCM drift RESOLVED 2026-07-29: it was never our curve. The IRS pillars are EUSA composites, which Bloomberg names EUR SWAP ANN (VS 6M), while S0201 is the vs-3M-EURIBOR surface - so the audit was booking the 3s6s basis as our error (+12.2bp at 1y1y tilting to -6.9bp at 20y10y). Forwards now read the EUSA year-pair securities (fwdTickerStyle yearPair: EUSA0101 = 1y1y, EUSA1515 = 15y15y), which price our curve to under 1bp: 1y1y -0.75, 5y5y -0.75, 10y10y +0.48. EUSA is better on three counts - right index, fewer stale days than S0201 (1-5 vs 9-15 over 2y), and EVERY year combination quoted with history where S0201 has a sparse start grid (S0201FS 11Y3Y has no data at all; EUSA1103 has 539 points). CAVEAT: EUSA liquidity varies - the round points are tight but odd long combinations can be very wide (EUSA1205 ~277bp) or have no market (EUSA0812, EUSA0930); unquoted points are bracket-interpolated, wide ones are used as-is. Do NOT revert to S0201. EUR OIS stays on S0514 (ESTR), which was always correct."
